sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "When the ratio between growth rates in different directions stays constant, an organism grows in bulk with little or no change of form; such constancy seldom lasts more than a season, and when the ratio alters the organism undergoes development, a steady and persistent change of form.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Constant growth-rate ratios preserve form; changing ratios produce development";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter III, “The Rate of Growth”; topic “Form as a function of growth rates”. Scope: growing multicellular organisms"
}
